<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Victor Schoelcher</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1804–1893</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Victor Schoelcher was a French politician and journalist who led the campaign to abolish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in the French colonies. As under-secretary for the colonies in the provisional government of the Second Republic, he drafted the decree of 27 April 1848 that abolished slavery throughout the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a>, freeing about 250,000 people in Guadeloupe, Martinique, Réunion and elsewhere. He opposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a>, lived in exile and later became a senator.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/victor-schoelcher.jpg" alt="Victor Schoelcher" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Henri Decaisne · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Victor_Sch%C5%93lcher_par_Henri_Decaisne.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
